# Result: fail

# Arrival Notes / Origin / Price

 * SDC, $499 (sale price)

 * Rather large, adult specimen.

 * Specimen came in 3 gallons of (pretty dirty) water

 * bag was lined with korean newspaper, I wonder if he was drop-shipped.

# Initial diagnoses

 * Has some ich visible, but a manageable amount

 * Belly is definitely pinched, which makes me very nervous

# Initial prognosis

 * While the pinched belly is pretty worrying, I'm optimistic.

# Day 1

 * Specimen arrived via AA cargo, after ~12 hours transit.

 * After drip acclimation, he was placed in a methylene blue + formalin bath for 30 minutes

 * After bath, placed in a tank by himself with air stone + PVC + sponge filter

 * Due to visible ich, I pre-dosed the water to 2.5 ppm copper + 300 mg nitrofurazone.

 * Fed brine shrimp soaked in metro / kanaplex. He ate somewhat well, but not super aggresively.

  - this inspired confidence, as he ate in full copper + ate medicated

  - video [here](https://photos.app.goo.gl/PUDg4L3TemN2zZT5A).

 * Due to SDC reputation for uronema, I dosed formalin at night

# Day 2

 * Eating less aggressively, possibly not at all.

 * Breathing seems labored, but not aggressive.

 * The water is somewhat cloudy, so I performed a 5 gallon water change, pre-dosed with
   copper + sucked out as much food as I could from the bottom

# Day 3

 * Still not eating much, but the food seems to disappear. I added nori on a clip
   to see if he'd go for that, but he seemed disinterested.

 * He swims around in a pattern: leave PVC towards the center, swim up a bit, turn around,
   swim through PVC, rotate (awkwardly) in the corner, and repeat.

# Day 4

 * Woke up around 6 AM for a frag swap, came down to find the tang dead.

 * He was floating at the top of the tank with his mouth facing up.

 * He was alive and looking normal when Ruxin added prazipro before we went to bed ~1 AM.
   I unfortunately didn't look at the fish before I went upstairs, as I was focused on the swap.

# Conclussions:

 * I'm not sure what happened, honestly.

 * I never saw any tang poops. I saw some stringy white excriment leaving him, but it was not
   long enough for me to think internal worms. Most likely internal flagellates.

 * Other than disease, ammonia could have played a factor. The fact that this guy died at night
   and was found at the surface could mean lack of O2, but I did not see unusual breathing from
   this guy, other than the somewhat labored breathes.

